# Copy to .env beside compose.yaml and fill in. .env is never committed.

# The domain whose DNS points at your server. Caddy gets its certificate.
SQUIDPDF_DOMAIN=pdf.example.com

# Basic auth: the name you sign in with, and your password's hash from
#   docker run --rm -it caddy:2.11.4 caddy hash-password
# Keep the single quotes: they stop the $ signs in the hash being read as variables.
SQUIDPDF_USER=me
SQUIDPDF_PASSWORD_HASH='$2a$14$paste.your.hash.here'

# The app's memory, and the space it saves exports in while it makes them, which counts
# inside it. It runs one worker a core of this server (nproc), each capped at 1 GiB
# (api/constants.py), and makes at most one export a worker. For N cores:
#   SQUIDPDF_APP_SCRATCH is N x 256m. An export is saved compressed, from a file of at most
#     100 MB (documents/constants.py); 256m leaves room for the fonts its edits add.
#   SQUIDPDF_APP_MEMORY is what this server can give the app: its memory, less Caddy's 512m
#     (compose.yaml) and room for the system: the app, Caddy and the system must fit in the
#     server's memory together. A limit past what it has never binds: the server runs out
#     first, and its own out-of-memory killer stops whichever process it picks, Caddy
#     included. Under a limit that binds, the kernel kills the app's largest process, most
#     often a worker at its cap: then only that worker's task fails, and the pool replaces
#     it (api/pool.py, what it guarantees). If it kills the app's main process instead,
#     every task in flight fails and the container restarts (restart: unless-stopped in
#     compose.yaml). Either is better than the server's own killer taking Caddy.
# The worst case the limit guards is N x 1792m plus 1024m, and a server that has it to spare
# can give it all. A worker's share is its 1 GiB, its export in /tmp (256m), and that export
# twice more in the server's process (512m): the worker sends the file back to the server
# (editing/export.py), which holds two copies while it reads it, then one until the reply is
# sent. The 1024m is the server's own process, and the replies it is still sending to slow
# readers after their workers move on. 256m and 1024m are starting values, not measurements.
# For a server of 4 GB and 2 cores: 3072m and 512m. Its worst case, 4608m, is more than it
# has; 3072m still holds both workers at their cap beside the server's 1024m, and leaves
# 512m for Caddy and, a starting value too, 512m for the system.
# Required: compose won't start the app without them.
SQUIDPDF_APP_MEMORY=
SQUIDPDF_APP_SCRATCH=
